#!/usr/bin/env bash
cat <<'TXT'
Git Bash Linux-Like Environment
===============================

Common commands supplied by a normal Git for Windows installation:
  ls cd pwd mkdir rmdir rm cp mv touch cat echo clear
  grep sed awk find sort uniq head tail wc cut xargs
  chmod ln tar gzip gunzip ssh scp sftp curl vim nano
  ps kill which env export history bash sh git

Added Linux-style compatibility commands:
  apt / apt-get   WinGet-backed package management when WinGet is available
  wget            Local wget-style wrapper using Git Bash's curl
  man             Show a command's --help output in less
  ifconfig        Windows network information
  ip              Limited: ip addr, ip link, ip route
  free            Memory summary
  open            Open a file/folder with Windows
  snap            Explains why Snap requires real Linux/WSL

Optional:
  jq              Install only if needed: apt install jq

Useful:
  apt update
  apt search python
  apt install make
  linux-check
  refreshpath

The base Git Bash setup does not require WinGet. WinGet is only used when
you explicitly use the apt compatibility command to install software.

This is Git Bash on Windows, not a Linux kernel. Linux-only features such as
systemctl, Snap packages, kernel modules and Linux service management require
WSL or a real Linux machine.
TXT
